# Public functions whose own doc-comment states a sentinel contract,
# per module. The `Result` migration's size, and its DIRECTION.
#
# `docs/error-model.md` sizes this with a `grep` proxy over
# `errno|sentinel|(- 0 1)` and tells the reader to recompute rather
# than quote. Recomputed 2026-08-26 that proxy reads 89 over 15 files,
# not the 64 recorded - and around sixty of those are COMMENT lines.
# `
#
# 2026-08-26, and the metric moved with the data: THE SIGNATURE
# DECIDES. A doc-comment mentioning `-errno` is prose, not a sentinel -
# `removeFile`'s explains which errno the kernel answers for a
# directory, and `IO.ioResult`'s explains the whole convention it
# converts AWAY from. Both counted on the comment alone, so porting a
# function to `Result` left it in the census: the number would have
# reported the migration making no progress while it made all of it.
# A declaration answering `Result` is not a sentinel whatever its
# comment says.
#
# WHAT THIS NUMBER WILL NOT REACH, and why a reader watching it fall
# should know: it counts sentinels of BOTH kinds. Of the thirteen left,
# eight report ABSENCE (`internFind`, `strFindByte`, `pathLastSlash`
# and friends answer -1 for "not found") and want `Option`, not
# `Result`. The five that report FAILURE are all decided already -
# `IO.writeStr` and the three `net` calls sit on hot paths where an
# `(Ok n)` block allocates per call or per poll wake, outside the
# per-request arena scope, and `check-net.sh` asserts memory ratios on
# exactly that server; `sysRandomNum` is a platform shim below `Err`.
#
# So this stops at thirteen until someone decides the `Option`
# migration. `docs/error-model.md` 10.1 has the measurement.
stdlib/Agent/Tags.ax 3
stdlib/IO.ax 1
stdlib/Intern.ax 1
stdlib/Path.ax 2
stdlib/Str.ax 2
